---
/**
 * website/src/components/Changelog.astro — the release entries (website plan, W1.9).
 *
 * One entry per release, newest first: the release's end-user summary, then the lines W0.6's
 * `src/lib/changelog.mjs` read out of `docs/releases/vX.Y.Z.md` at build time, in a block the
 * reader opens. The component writes no prose of its own: the tag comes from the filename,
 * the summary from `website/data/release-summaries.yaml`, the lines and the sections are the
 * file's own, and the only words it adds are the notes block's label and the "not yet
 * released" marker. Every heading inside an entry is a `###` heading the file itself carries
 * — `### For operators`, `### Known limits` — printed as the heading element it is, so a
 * section the format allows is a section the page renders.
 *
 * Five decisions are worth stating rather than leaving in the code:
 *
 *   1. **No date.** The release files carry no date, the site's build is network-free, and
 *      the date a reader wants is the one the GitHub release states, so each heading links
 *      to it (D100).
 *   2. **The summary is the site's data, and the release file's operator detail is one click
 *      away** (the owner's brief of 2026-10-19). A reader who lands on a changelog of fifteen
 *      releases wants to know what each one gives them; the Highlights bullets below are
 *      written for the operator who runs Fugaro, so they are kept, collapsed, rather than
 *      shown first. The summary is a file in this repository — `website/data/`, read through
 *      the content pipeline — so the page still writes nothing of its own, and a release with
 *      no summary stops the build rather than rendering an entry with nothing under its
 *      heading.
 *   3. **The file's own line structure is kept.** A line the file writes as a bullet
 *      becomes a list item and any other line becomes a paragraph of its own, so a note
 *      that is prose does not gain a bullet and a list does not lose one.
 *   4. **The file's inline markup is rendered, not printed.** `` `code` `` becomes a
 *      `<code>` element and `**bold**` a `<strong>` one: the markers are markup and not
 *      text, and printing them would put backticks in front of a reader. The files carry
 *      no link, no image and no other construct — the generator hands the lines over as
 *      written — so this is the whole of the inline rendering, and it is applied to text
 *      that is escaped first.
 *   5. **A release the plugin's version has not reached is not a release that happened.**
 *      `released` is false for a release file written ahead of its version bump in
 *      `plugin/.claude-plugin/plugin.json`. The entry says so rather than reading as
 *      shipped, and the flag is a file in this repository rather than a tag of the checkout
 *      the build runs in, which the deploy checkout does not carry (the owner's brief of
 *      2026-10-19).
 *
 * The notes block is a `<details>`, so it opens and closes with no JavaScript: the site's
 * pages are readable and usable with the one script off (D183).
 *
 * **The entries are cards, and that is the bold look** (the owner's approved home page,
 * `website/design-ref/HomeV3.dc.html`, applied to this page by the owner's brief of
 * 2026-10-21). Each release is one `base.css` `.card` — the panel the boards draw everything
 * on, one step above the ground with a hairline and the larger radius — and the four things
 * inside it are the entry's hierarchy rather than four paragraphs of one size: the tag is the
 * card's title at the display scale's middle step (`display-md`), the summary is the loudest
 * prose in the card, the collapsed notes are a control a reader can see and reach (the accent's
 * hairline, a target the owner's brief of 2026-10-19 measures every control against), and the
 * file's own lines under it are the quietest tone. The list of them is a grid with a gap of a
 * spacing step rather than the hairline-separated stack the page used to be, so two releases
 * read as two things rather than as one long list. **No word of any of it changes**: the classes
 * are markup and the styles are layout, and `website/test/changelog-page.mjs` still holds every
 * sentence on the page to a release file or to `website/data/release-summaries.yaml`.
 */

/**
 * One section of a release file's body, as W0.6's generator returns it: a `###` heading the
 * format names (`### For operators`, `### Known limits`), its words a title, and the lines
 * under it verbatim. The two headings are the same kind of thing, so a release carries a
 * list of these rather than a field per heading, and a third one later is a room in the list
 * rather than a fourth branch here.
 */
interface Section {
  /** The heading's own words, with the `###` left behind: `For operators`, `Known limits`. */
  title: string
  /** The lines under the heading, verbatim. */
  lines: string[]
}

/** One release, as W0.6's generator returns it: the file's own text and nothing else. */
interface Entry {
  /** The tag, from the filename: `vX.Y.Z`. */
  tag: string
  /** The tag without its `v`, which is the version the filename carries. */
  version: string
  /** True unless the release file is ahead of the plugin's own version. */
  released: boolean
  /** The file's lines before its first section heading, verbatim. */
  highlights: string[]
  /** Every section the file writes after them, in the file's own order. */
  sections: Section[]
}

interface Props {
  entries: Entry[]
  /**
   * Each release's end-user summary, keyed by its tag: `website/data/release-summaries.yaml`,
   * read through the content pipeline (W1.3), so this component types no summary of its own.
   */
  summaries: { [tag: string]: string }
  /**
   * The repository every entry links its GitHub release at: site.yaml's own fact (W0.2),
   * so the page builds the URL and never types it.
   */
  repository: string
}

const { entries, summaries, repository } = Astro.props

/** One element of an entry's body, in the order the file writes it. */
type Element = { kind: 'list'; items: string[] } | { kind: 'paragraph'; text: string } | { kind: 'heading'; text: string }

/**
 * The text of a line, escaped for HTML. The release notes carry `<` in their own prose
 * (`fugaro init --firebase <firebase-project-id>`, `--repo <owner/name>`), which is text
 * to a reader and must not become an element in the page.
 */
function escapeHTML(text: string): string {
  return text.replace(/&/g, '&amp;').replace(/</g, '&lt;').replace(/>/g, '&gt;')
}

/**
 * The file's inline markup, rendered: `code` as `<code>`, **bold** as `<strong>`. The
 * text is escaped before the markers are turned into elements, so a `<` in a code span
 * stays a `<`.
 */
function inline(text: string): string {
  return escapeHTML(text)
    .replace(/`([^`]+)`/g, '<code>$1</code>')
    .replace(/\*\*([^*]+)\*\*/g, '<strong>$1</strong>')
}

/**
 * The lines as the elements a reader sees: consecutive bullets become one list, and every
 * other line is a paragraph of its own, with the bullet marker left to the list that
 * renders it and everything else kept as written (an operator step's own `1.`, for one).
 */
function elements(lines: string[]): Element[] {
  const out: Element[] = []
  for (const line of lines) {
    const bullet = /^[-*]\s+(.*)$/.exec(line)
    if (bullet !== null) {
      const last = out[out.length - 1]
      if (last !== undefined && last.kind === 'list') last.items.push(bullet[1])
      else out.push({ kind: 'list', items: [bullet[1]] })
      continue
    }
    out.push({ kind: 'paragraph', text: line })
  }
  return out
}

/**
 * An entry's whole body: its highlights, then each section the file writes — the operator
 * steps, the release's known limits — as its own heading with its lines under it, in the
 * order the file writes them. The heading's words are the file's own, printed as the
 * heading element they are; the file's order is kept rather than the format's, so a page
 * never reorders what its source said.
 */
function body(entry: Entry): Element[] {
  const out = elements(entry.highlights)
  for (const section of entry.sections) {
    out.push({ kind: 'heading', text: section.title })
    out.push(...elements(section.lines))
  }
  return out
}

/**
 * A release's summary, out of the site's own data. A release with no entry there stops the
 * build: the summary is the line a reader reads first, and an entry with nothing under its
 * heading is the hole this component exists to refuse.
 */
function summaryOf(entry: Entry): string {
  const summary = summaries[entry.tag]
  if (summary === undefined) {
    throw new Error(
      `docs/releases/${entry.tag}.md has no entry in website/data/release-summaries.yaml, and the changelog renders that file's line under the release's heading (the owner's brief of 2026-10-19).`,
    )
  }
  return summary
}
---

<div class="changelog">
  {entries.map((entry) => (
    /* The entry is a card: the board's panel, so two releases are two boxes rather than one
       list, and `website/test/changelog-page.mjs` holds this element to the class. */
    <article class="release card">
      {/* The tag is the entry's own heading and its anchor, and it links to the GitHub
          release: the one place a date and the artifacts are stated (D100). It is the card's
          title at the display scale's middle step, and the `id` stays the first attribute
          because it is what the entry is linked by. */}
      <h2 id={entry.tag} class="release-tag display-md">
        <a href={`${repository}/releases/tag/${entry.tag}`} rel="noopener">{entry.tag}</a>
      </h2>

      {/* Not a release that happened: the file is on main, and the plugin's version has not
          reached it yet (the owner's brief of 2026-10-19). It is a pill — the board's shape for
          a badge of one or two words — and the warning tone, because it is the one line of the
          card that says "not yet". */}
      {!entry.released && <p class="release-status pill" data-status="not-yet-released">not yet released</p>}

      {/* The summary a reader reads first: the site's own data, and the one line of the entry
          that is not a line of the release file. The tag is carried as an attribute so the
          page's own gate can hold each summary to its entry in the data file. */}
      <p class="release-summary" data-release-summary={entry.tag}>{summaryOf(entry)}</p>

      {/* The release file's own lines, for the operator who runs Fugaro: collapsed, so a
          reader who wants the product news reads the summaries and nothing else, and the
          detail is one click away. A `<details>` needs no script. */}
      <details class="release-notes">
        <summary>Highlights</summary>
        {body(entry).map((element) =>
          element.kind === 'list' ? (
            <ul>
              {element.items.map((item) => (
                <li set:html={inline(item)}></li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          ) : element.kind === 'heading' ? (
            <h3>{element.text}</h3>
          ) : (
            <p set:html={inline(element.text)}></p>
          ),
        )}
      </details>
    </article>
  ))}
</div>

<style>
  /* The bold look's shapes, and the entry's own hierarchy (the owner's brief of 2026-10-21).
     The spacing, the type scale, the radii and the tones come from W1.2's tokens and from the
     card, pill and display classes `base.css` writes once for the site; this block adds the
     layout of the list and the four sizes an entry is read in, and **names no colour of its
     own** — every tone below is a palette token, so `website/test/a11y.mjs` can compute the
     pair it is read against. */

  /* The releases, one card each: a grid with a step of the spacing scale between two of them,
     which is what makes two releases read as two things rather than as one long list (the
     hairline-separated stack this page used to be). The step is a token and not a number, so
     `website/test/changelog-page.mjs` can read it back and the page's room is comparable with
     the room the rest of the site gives. */
  .changelog {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-lg);
  }

  /* The card's own column: the title, the status pill, the summary and the notes, one gap
     apart, each at the card's own padding (`.card` in `base.css`). Every child's own margin is
     dropped, because the gap is the whole of the room between them — a paragraph's default
     bottom margin on top of the gap would be a second one. */
  .release {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .release > * {
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* The pill is inline-flex, and a grid item is stretched to the column's width: without this
     the "not yet released" badge would run the width of the card instead of hugging its words. */
  .release-status {
    justify-self: start;
    color: var(--warn);
  }

  /* The summary: the loudest prose in the card, one step up the type scale from the body, and
     the page's own measure (which every paragraph gets from `base.css`). It is the line a
     reader reads first, so it is set at the size a line read first is set at. */
  .release-summary {
    font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
  }

  /* The file's own lines under the notes: the quietest tone in the card, because they are the
     operator's detail rather than what the release gives a reader (the owner's brief of
     2026-10-19). */
  .release-notes {
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  /* The notes' control: the board's chip — the accent's hairline, the larger radius and the
     accent's legible tone — at a target the owner's brief of 2026-10-19 measures every control
     against. The disclosure marker is the browser's own (the element keeps its default
     `display`), so the block still says whether it is open with no markup of ours. */
  .release-notes summary {
    box-sizing: border-box;
    min-height: 44px;
    padding: var(--space-xs) var(--space-md);
    border: var(--border-width) solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    color: var(--accent-on-dark);
    font-size: var(--font-size-base);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
  }

  .release-notes h3 {
    margin-block: var(--space-md) var(--space-2xs);
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: var(--font-size-base);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-tight);
  }

  .release-notes ul {
    padding-inline-start: var(--space-md);
  }

  .release-notes li,
  .release-notes p {
    line-height: var(--leading-normal);
  }

  /* A code span inside the card is drawn on the ground rather than on the surface it is
     already sitting on (the card is the surface, `base.css`): the same tone the page's own
     ground is, so a command in a release note still reads as a chip rather than as a word in
     the mono face. `--text` on `--ground` is a pair the contrast check computes.

     **`:global()` around the element, because the notes arrive through `set:html`.** The
     release file's lines are markup this component did not write (`inline()` above renders
     them), and Astro appends the component's `data-astro-cid-…` to **every** compound of a
     scoped selector: `.release-notes code` was built as `.release-notes[cid] code[cid]`, and
     a `<code>` a release note carries has no `cid` for the second compound to match. The rule
     was dead — every chip in the notes kept `base.css`'s own `--surface`, which is the card's
     own tone, so the one thing this rule exists for did not happen — and it read as a rule
     that was there. `:global()` opts the chip's own compound out of the scoping and leaves
     `.release-notes` scoped to this component, so the rule reaches the chips of this page and
     nothing else on the site; `website/test/changelog-page.mjs` now reads whether the rule
     can reach a chip rather than whether a rule with those fragments exists, and refuses one
     that cannot. */
  .release-notes :global(code) {
    background: var(--ground);
  }
</style>
